---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';

const LOCKED_PROFILE = `{
  "meta": { "name": "ouijit-myapp-1a2b3c" },
  "extends": ["ouijit"],
  "network": {
    "allow_domain": ["*"],
    "deny_domain": ["pastebin.com", "*.ngrok.io"],
    "open_port": [5173, 5432],
    "credentials": ["github"],
    "custom_credentials": {
      "github": {
        "upstream": "https://api.github.com",
        "credential_key": "cmd://github",
        "env_var": "GITHUB_TOKEN",
        "credential_format": "token {}"
      }
    }
  },
  "credential_capture": {
    "github": { "command": ["gh", "auth", "token"], "timeout_secs": 10, "cache_ttl_secs": 900 }
  },
  "filesystem": {
    "allow": ["$HOME/.local/state/nono-ssh"],
    "unix_socket": ["$HOME/.local/state/nono-ssh/agent.sock"]
  },
  "environment": {
    "set_vars": {
      "SSH_AUTH_SOCK": "$HOME/.local/state/nono-ssh/agent.sock",
      "GIT_SSH_COMMAND": "ssh -o ProxyCommand='python3 -I $HOME/.local/state/nono-ssh/connect.py %h %p' -o UserKnownHostsFile=$HOME/.local/state/nono-ssh/known_hosts -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new"
    }
  }
}`;

const SSH_HELPER = String.raw`import base64, os, socket, sys, threading, urllib.parse
host, port = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
p = urllib.parse.urlparse(os.environ["HTTPS_PROXY"])
s = socket.create_connection((p.hostname, p.port))
auth = base64.b64encode(f"{p.username}:{p.password}".encode()).decode()
s.sendall(f"CONNECT {host}:{port} HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: {host}:{port}\r\nProxy-Authorization: Basic {auth}\r\n\r\n".encode())
reply = b""
while b"\r\n\r\n" not in reply:
    chunk = s.recv(1)
    if not chunk: sys.exit(1)
    reply += chunk
if b" 200 " not in reply.split(b"\r\n")[0]:
    sys.stderr.write(reply.split(b"\r\n")[0].decode() + "\n"); sys.exit(1)
def up():
    while (d := sys.stdin.buffer.read1(65536)): s.sendall(d)
    s.shutdown(socket.SHUT_WR)
threading.Thread(target=up, daemon=True).start()
while (d := s.recv(65536)): sys.stdout.buffer.write(d); sys.stdout.buffer.flush()`;
---
<Docs slug="sandbox">
  <h1>Sandbox</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Run an agent with its permission checks off without handing it your machine. Ouijit's sandbox limits a
    command to its task's worktree, and you choose what runs inside it: a whole terminal, a hook, or one command.
  </p>

  <h2>Where things run</h2>
  <p>
    Each project has a <strong>Default</strong> under <strong>Project Settings &rarr; Sandbox</strong>: Host,
    Sandbox, or Custom. Task terminals, hooks, Run, and scripts all run there unless something more specific
    says otherwise:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One terminal.</strong> Right-click a task and use <strong>Resume in</strong>. It lists every place, with the default checked. Plain <strong>Resume</strong> uses the default. When there is only one other place, the item names it, as in <strong>Resume in Sandbox</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>One run of a hook.</strong> The dialog that appears when you move a task starts on the hook's setting and can change it for that run.</li>
    <li><strong>One Run or script.</strong> <strong>Run in</strong> at the top of a terminal's <strong>+</strong> menu.</li>
    <li><strong>A hook, every time.</strong> Each of start, continue, review, and done has its own setting, which starts on Default. Set it when a hook must always run somewhere else, such as a done hook that pushes with credentials only the host has.</li>
    <li><strong>Part of a command.</strong> <code>ouijit sandbox -- &lt;command&gt;</code> runs just that command in the sandbox, from a host terminal. See <a href="#command">below</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A sandboxed terminal carries an outlined status dot and a suffix on its label, and so does a host terminal
    while a command started with <code>ouijit sandbox</code> is running. A sandboxed runner's tab dot is
    outlined too.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the chosen sandbox cannot start, the terminal says why and Ouijit raises an error. It never runs the
    command on the host instead, and a restored terminal whose sandbox is gone fails the same way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="command">Sandboxing one command</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ouijit sandbox</code> runs a single command in the sandbox with the same access its task's terminal
    would have, while the rest of the line stays on the host. A start hook can pull on the host and contain only the
    agent:
  </p>
  <pre><code>git pull origin main &amp;&amp; ouijit sandbox -- claude --dangerously-skip-permissions "$OUIJIT_TASK_NAME"</code></pre>
  <p>
    Add <code>--custom</code> to use the Custom sandbox. The command gets an API token of its own at the
    project's <a href="#cli">CLI access</a> level, never the host terminal's, and loses it when it exits. A host
    step that runs after a sandboxed one can still execute files the agent changed, such as tests it edited. The
    sandbox protects your machine while the agent works, rather than from what it leaves behind.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cli">The Ouijit CLI inside a sandbox</h2>
  <p>
    Set it under <strong>Project Settings &rarr; Sandbox &rarr; Ouijit CLI access</strong>. At every level a
    sandboxed command edits only the comment drafts a sandbox wrote, never yours:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>View</strong>, the default. Read only, in its own project. A sandboxed command can read its own task, its diff notes and pull requests, draft pull request and Linear comments, print links to places in Ouijit, and ask you to run a command. On its own terminal it can read the screen, add tags, read panels, open markdown files from its worktree, run scripts and open shells in runners inside its own sandbox, and open previews of localhost. A runner or shell anywhere else, or a preview of any other page, waits for you to approve it, as an ask does.</li>
    <li><strong>Own task.</strong> It can also move, rename and describe its own task, link it to a pull request or issue, and add to-do tasks. It can read every sandboxed terminal on its task. Moving a task runs that column's hook as usual, but a hook that would run on the host waits for you to open it, even with <code>--run-hook</code>. A one-off <code>--hook-command</code> or <code>--sandbox</code> is refused.</li>
    <li><strong>Whole board.</strong> It can start, move and message tasks across your projects, but not change hooks, scripts, or how tasks open, start their worktrees and are sandboxed. The tasks, terminals and chats it starts, and the hooks they run, run in its own sandbox whatever the project says, and it can message, answer or interrupt only the agents running there. Anything that would run outside its sandbox waits for you.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Sandboxed Chat has its own setting, under <strong>Settings &rarr; Chat</strong>, with two levels.
    <strong>Whole board</strong>, the default, is as above, under Chat's profile or launcher, with each task it
    starts writing only its own worktree. At <strong>View</strong> it can read every project and follow tasks and
    chats, but not move a task or message an agent.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chat, like any sandboxed agent, can steer only the agents running in its own sandbox: steering an agent means
    using its access.
  </p>

  <h2>Ouijit's sandbox</h2>
  <p>
    Ouijit's sandbox is <a href="https://github.com/nolabs-ai/nono" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nono</a>,
    which runs a command in place on the host worktree under kernel-level access limits (Seatbelt on macOS,
    Landlock on Linux). It is a kernel access policy on a host process rather than a separate machine, so it
    starts instantly with no file sync.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ouijit runs nono's default profile and nono's own agent packs unchanged. It adds only what a task needs: its
    worktree, the repository's git history, a package cache, and the <code>ouijit</code> CLI. Everything else,
    including whether the network is open, comes from the profile, which you own and can edit.
  </p>
  <h3>What it grants</h3>
  <p>
    Filesystem access is limited to the worktree and the repository's git data. <code>.git</code> is read-only
    apart from what the task's own commits write: <code>objects/</code>, the task's entry under
    <code>worktrees/</code>, and its own branch's ref and reflog. Other branches, other tasks' worktrees,
    <code>hooks/</code> and <code>config</code> stay read-only, and a merge refuses if the branch it lands on
    moved while the task ran by anything but git on your machine.
    Ouijit also grants a per-project package cache and keeps its status port reachable, so agent status reports
    back even when the network is blocked. All of this is derived from the task at spawn time.
  </p>
  <h3>The profile</h3>
  <p>
    Everything else is a nono profile file. Each project gets its own, at
    <code>~/.config/nono/profiles/ouijit-&lt;project&gt;-&lt;id&gt;.json</code>, written once to extend Ouijit's
    base profile, which lets Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, and Pi sign in and run, and then left to you. Open it
    from <strong>Sandbox profile</strong> in settings to block the network, open ports, or grant folders, in
    nono's own <a href="https://nono.sh/docs/cli/features/profile-authoring" target="_blank"
    rel="noopener">profile format</a>. <strong>Change&hellip;</strong> points the project at another profile
    file, such as one your team shares, and <strong>Reset</strong> goes back to the project's own. Profiles live
    outside the repository, where a sandboxed agent cannot edit its own permissions.
  </p>

  <h3 id="locking-down">Locking it down</h3>
  <p>
    Out of the box the sandbox limits files. The network stays open, and the agent can reach your SSH agent and
    keychain. This profile closes off the hosts you name, keeps your GitHub token out of the agent's hands, and
    keeps local servers and git over SSH working. Open it from <strong>Sandbox profile</strong> in settings and add
    what you need. We tested it with the nono Ouijit bundles, on macOS.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{LOCKED_PROFILE}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hosts.</strong> <code>allow_domain: ["*"]</code> with <code>deny_domain</code> lets everything through except the hosts denied. List hosts in place of <code>"*"</code> to allow only those.</li>
    <li><strong>GitHub token.</strong> nono runs <code>gh auth token</code> outside the sandbox and adds the token to requests to api.github.com on their way out. The agent's <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> is a stand-in that is useless anywhere else. Every request from inside to api.github.com goes out with your token, so add <code>endpoint_rules</code> to limit what it can call. curl, Node, Python and git pick this up. Go tools such as <code>gh</code> don't trust nono's proxy certificate by default.</li>
    <li><strong>Local ports.</strong> <code>open_port</code> lets the agent reach a dev server or database on your machine.</li>
    <li><strong>Git over SSH.</strong> ssh can't use an HTTP proxy, so <code>GIT_SSH_COMMAND</code> tunnels through nono's with a small helper. Save it as <code>~/.local/state/nono-ssh/connect.py</code>. macOS gives your SSH agent a new path at each login, so keep a link to it current from your shell profile.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>ln -sf "$SSH_AUTH_SOCK" ~/.local/state/nono-ssh/agent.sock</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{SSH_HELPER}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Any domain rule or credential route sends the sandbox's traffic through nono's proxy. From then on only the
    ports you open reach your machine, plain ssh stops (hence the helper), and your SSH agent needs the socket
    grant above.
  </p>

  <h3>Requirements</h3>
  <p>Ouijit bundles the <code>nono</code> binary and the agent packs it uses. There is nothing extra to install.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>macOS</strong>. Seatbelt (<code>sandbox-exec</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Linux</strong>. Landlock, which needs kernel 5.13 or newer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="custom">Custom</h2>
  <p>
    The Custom backend runs a task's terminals under a launcher you provide: a reviewed script in your team's
    control, with its own pinned sandbox binary, committed policy, and preflight checks. Ouijit grants nothing
    itself. The launcher owns the boundary. Set the command under <strong>Project Settings &rarr; Sandbox &rarr;
    Custom launcher</strong> or with <code>ouijit settings sandbox set --launcher</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Don't start the launcher by hand from a task terminal. That terminal's <code>OUIJIT_API_TOKEN</code> has
    host access and would go into the sandbox with the launcher. An agent inside could then rewrite hooks and
    this setting so the next spawn runs on the host. Configure the launcher here, or use <code>ouijit sandbox
    --custom -- &lt;command&gt;</code>, which gives the command a sandbox-scoped token of its own.
  </p>

  <h3>What Ouijit passes</h3>
  <p>
    Ouijit runs <code>&lt;launcher&gt; [args] -- &lt;shell&gt; [shell args]</code> with the working directory set
    to the task worktree and the full terminal environment: <code>OUIJIT_API_URL</code> and a
    <strong>sandbox-scoped</strong> <code>OUIJIT_API_TOKEN</code>, <code>OUIJIT_PTY_ID</code>,
    <code>OUIJIT_WRAPPER_DIR</code>, <code>OUIJIT_CLI_PATH</code> (when the bundled CLI is present),
    <code>OUIJIT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR</code>,
    and the shell integration's <code>ZDOTDIR</code>. The launcher's argv is exactly the configured command,
    split on whitespace with shell-style quoting. Nothing is expanded except a leading <code>~/</code> on the
    launcher path, and nothing is added except the <code>--</code> and the shell.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything Ouijit would otherwise have granted is exported as advisory hints. They are computed on the host
    from the project path and Ouijit's own directories, never from the worktree, and a launcher is free to ignore
    them.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>What Ouijit's other backends do with it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_WORKTREE</code></td><td>The task worktree, read+write.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_GIT_DIR</code></td><td>The repository's <code>.git</code>, read-only. Unset for a folder that is not a repository.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_GIT_WRITABLE_DIRS</code></td><td>Colon-separated directories under it to make writable: <code>objects</code>, and the task's entry under <code>worktrees</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_GIT_WRITABLE_FILES</code></td><td>Colon-separated files under it to make writable: the task branch's ref, its <code>.lock</code>, its reflog, and <code>packed-refs.lock</code>. Empty outside a task.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_HOOK_PORT</code></td><td>The localhost port that must stay reachable for agent status to report back.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_CACHE_DIR</code></td><td>A per-project package-manager cache outside any worktree, read+write. Shared by every task of the project.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_WRAPPER_DIR</code></td><td>Ouijit's agent shims and CLI reference, read-only. Its <code>bin/</code> is first on <code>PATH</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_CLI_DIR</code></td><td>The bundled <code>ouijit</code> CLI the shim runs, read-only. Set only when the bundled CLI is present.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>What the launcher is responsible for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Exec the argv after <code>--</code> with the inherited environment intact: at least <code>PATH</code>, <code>ZDOTDIR</code>, every <code>OUIJIT_*</code> variable, <code>TERM</code>, <code>HOME</code>, <code>SHELL</code>, and the locale. Shell integration, the status dot, and the <code>ouijit</code> and agent shims are all carried by the environment. A launcher that scrubs it starts a bare shell.</li>
    <li>Own the boundary. Ouijit adds no <code>--allow</code> or <code>--read</code> flags.</li>
    <li>Keep the hook port reachable if you block the network.</li>
    <li>Exit non-zero to refuse. Whatever the launcher wrote to stderr appears in the terminal, the exit line prints
    red, and Ouijit raises a "sandbox failed to start" notice. Ouijit never falls back to an unsandboxed
    shell.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Where the launcher may live</h3>
  <p>
    An absolute path or a bare name resolved on <code>PATH</code>. Relative paths are refused, and so is any
    absolute path inside the worktree or the spawn directory. The sandboxed agent can write there, and a
    launcher it can edit is one it can turn off. Keep a versioned launcher in the repo if you like, but install
    it from a reviewed checkout to somewhere the sandbox cannot write, such as <code>~/.local/bin</code>, and
    point the setting there. If your <code>PATH</code> contains relative entries, use an absolute path.
  </p>
</Docs>
